7231 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186411117 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157231 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186571378 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157231 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186571407 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157231 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying Logically Equivalent Statements" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157231 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186157012 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157231 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "bc5b8688-3838-5e9e-9304-9a4c787034ee" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157478 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186158012 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157478 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186158013 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157478 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186158014 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157478 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186158015 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157478 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157478 #edb/ref 1045 "Logical Equivalence" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157478 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186158012 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157478 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050777 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157478 #edb/ref 1050 780.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157478 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "d786bc7e-7381-59dc-826e-1b785938d470" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157794 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186477700 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186157794 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186477701 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186157794 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186477702 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186157794 #edb/ref 1069 "q-81053" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157794 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157794 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following statements are logically equivalent?\n- **I.** $¬A∧B$\n- **II.** $¬(A∨¬B)$\n- **III.** $A∨¬B$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186157794 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7074b983-3ace-5190-bf87-4742f8c8239b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157794 #edb/ref 1074 "Two statements are logically equivalent if they have the same truth table. So, we construct a truth table for the three given statements:\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,B\\,$ | $\\,¬A∧B\\,$ | $\\,¬(A∨¬B)\\,$ | $\\,A∨¬B\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ |\n\nWe see that only the statements $¬A∧B$ and $¬(A∨¬B)$ have the same truth table, so they are logically equivalent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158012 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186158013 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158012 #edb/ref 1061 120201 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158012 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c35dc71e-cbb8-5b1e-9e21-1319c9031864" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158012 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186158579 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158013 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186158014 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158013 #edb/ref 1061 120202 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158013 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c619f370-2162-5e52-83a2-7a22517f0f26" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158013 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186157229 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158014 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186158015 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158014 #edb/ref 1061 120205 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158014 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5765c4ca-7ca3-542f-b314-a0b02c46501b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158014 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186157230 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158015 #edb/ref 1061 120208 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158015 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1c1388e3-4999-5448-a4d6-93e79c6a1bf2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158015 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186157231 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158579 #edb/ref 1064 "Two statements are **logically equivalent** if they have identical truth values in their respective truth tables. In this lesson, we'll use truth tables to show that two complex compound statements are logically equivalent. \n\nIf two statements $P$ and $Q$ are logically equivalent, we write\n\n$$\nP\\equiv Q\n$$\n\nwhere the symbol \"$\\equiv$\" means \"is logically equivalent to.\"\n\nFor example, let's use a truth table to prove the following logical equivalence:\n\n$$\n(A \\land \\neg A) \\lor B \\equiv B\n$$\n\nFirst, we construct truth tables with columns $A$ and $B$ containing all possible combinations of truth values. Then, we create another column for $\\neg A,$ which has the opposite truth values to $A.$\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,B\\,$ | $\\, \\neg A \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ |\n| $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ |\n| $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ |\n| $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ |\n\nNow, we add a column for $A \\land \\neg A.$ Recall that $A\\land \\neg A$ is true if $A$ is true and $\\neg A$ is true and is false otherwise. In this case, $A\\land \\neg A$ is always false.\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,B\\,$ | $\\, \\neg A \\,$ | $\\, A \\land \\neg A \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ |\n| $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ |\n| $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ |\n| $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ |\n\nFinally, we complete the table with a column for $(A \\land \\neg A) \\lor B.$ Recall that $(A \\land \\neg A) \\lor B$ is true if $(A \\land \\neg A)$ is true, or $B$ is true, or both are true. \n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,B\\,$ | $\\, \\neg A \\,$ | $\\, A \\land \\neg A \\,$ | $\\, (A \\land \\neg A) \\lor B \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ |\n\nWe see that the truth values of $(A \\land \\neg A) \\lor B$ are the same as the truth values of $B.$ This proves that $(A \\land \\neg A) \\lor B$ is logically equivalent to $B.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158579 #edb/ref 1065 8260 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158579 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "52050e7d-e6dd-5b8e-a1f9-56a8c00bbaa7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158579 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186376279 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186376280 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186376279 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186376283 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186376279 #edb/ref 1068 